t think  anybody was prepared properly  in the early part of the pandemic but  we're past those early days  and things have changed dramatically for  nurse ellis  yes we'd like you to be here 5 30 a.m  tomorrow morning well she's back in her  original role  in the pre-operative department after  six weeks in the icu last spring  and across the hospital the number of  coveted patients is down  substantially let us get back to the  things we normally do which is taking  care of all the other medical problems  that people of brooklyn have  dr joshua rosenberg is the hospital's  infection control director  and he's an icu physician last year he  took us through one of their three  coded icus so this is the one-year  checkup  yeah what's the evaluation i'm grateful  how well we've survived  we had no clue what we were getting into  that but what's interesting is that  as all the data has come out through the  various research studies  a lot of the stuff we were doing back  then they're recommending now  and i'm still looking at this saying  it's not completely done yet nurse ellis  feels the same way about the virus that  it's not over yet and she says she will  never forget the fear  of those first days patients died  much more rapidly and often than i had  ever seen in my life  what was your worst day i think  every day was a challenging day we were  afraid  it was business as usual professionally  but it wasn't business as usual mentally  and emotionally  what didn't we see that went on behind  the scenes the sadness  i think the tears maybe even the  aggravation  and the frustration with what the  unknown  covet 19 has been the virus of the  unknown  the day after nurse ellis said to me  last april i want what you have  i sent her my face shield right after we  left the hospital our team agreed  it's the right thing to do she told me  she wore it every day at work  for all of the six weeks she spent in  the icu and  she never she never really forgot that  moment of  of seeing somebody walking in as a guest  of the hospital  better dressed than she was right so she  never got covered by the way that's good  and she's fully vaccinated you must feel  good to know that david i remember that  story very well  when i saw you in that in that getup i  thought god that does seem  a lot but it was very alarming because  nobody knew what we were dealing with  exactly including the medical community  it's hard to look at those pictures  realize even then in real time we didn't  know what is happening here  just remember the key is they made a lot  of progress last year it was such a  scary time to send on that note made a  lot of progress thank you david good to  see you
 

